  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nConfidence remains high that hot and humid conditions will\nreturn tomorrow and persist through mid-week as a ridge axis\nbuilds across the Great Lakes over the next 24 hours. As a\nresult, temperatures will climb into the upper-80s and low-90s\nacross the region, with at least three consecutive days of hot\nand humid weather expected through Wednesday. The probability of\ntemperatures reaching the low-90s ranges from 40-90% during this\ntime period, with probabilities trending higher especially on\nWednesday. \n\nOvernight temperatures will provide little relief, with lows\ngenerally remaining in the upper-60s and low-70s. This\ncombination of warm daytime temperatures and limited overnight\ncooling will contribute to increasing heat stress. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nClouds will increase today ahead of an approaching shortwave.\nThis wave is progged to track southeast across the Lower Great\nLakes region tonight into Monday on the northeast periphery of \nthe ridge, with shower and possible thunderstorm chances north \nof Pittsburgh. Another wave is possible late Tuesday, resulting\nin shower and thunderstorm chances mainly near and north of\nI-80. Extent and exact location of POPs will be dependent on \nthe strength and northern extent of the ridge. \n\nThe ridge is progged to retrograde to the west from mid to late\nweek, resulting in NW flow aloft across the Upper Ohio Valley\nregion. Embedded shortwaves in the flow should result in\nincreasing shower and thunderstorm chances area wide, \nespecially late Wednesday into Thursday. With instability and \nincreasing flow aloft increasing shear, severe thunderstorms \nwill be possible. Machine-learning guidance and SPC outlooks \nindicate the late Wednesday through Thursday would be the \ngreatest potential for strong to severe storms. Uncertainty \nremains in the exact details of convection timing and placement,\nand depends on the breakdown of the ridge upstream. \n\nThe probability of severe weather will likely decrease late week\n(Friday into Sunday), as deep trough develops across the East \nCoast. With this noted pattern, temperatures will likely return \nto near-normal opposed to above-average.",
